--TEST--
`Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::attributes` names every attach site a declaration carries and `Core\Reflect\AttributeInfo` says which declaration each is written on — `rule:core-classes/reflect` and `rule:attributes/attach-sites-and-forms`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Reflect\AttributeInfo::name
// covers: Core\Reflect\AttributeInfo::target
// covers: Core\Reflect\AttributeInfo::parameter
// covers: Core\Reflect\AttributeInfo::fields
// covers: Core\Reflect\AttributeInfo::field
// `rule:attributes/attach-sites-and-forms` fixes four sites, and the roster
// carries all four: the class's own, a property's, a method's and a
// parameter's. The pair `target`/`parameter` is what tells the last two apart —
// an attribute above a method and one before its parameter both name the
// method, and only one of them names a parameter.
type Route = {path: string};

#[Route(path: "/users")]
#[{audit: true}]
class Controller {
    #[{column: "name"}]
    public string $name = "";

    #[Route(path: "/show")]
    public function show(#[{inject: "repo"}] int $id): int {
        return $id;
    }
}

var $described = Core\Reflect::forObject(new Controller());
var $roster = $described->attributes();

// The count is asserted beside the rows because a roster that dropped the
// parameter's site still prints four plausible lines. Both forms are here for
// the same reason: the name is what was *written*, and the bare form's empty
// string is an answer rather than an omission.
echo Core\Arr::count($roster), "\n";
foreach ($roster as Core\Reflect\AttributeInfo $attribute) {
    echo $attribute->name() == "" ? "bare" : $attribute->name(), " on ",
         $attribute->target() == "" ? "<class>" : $attribute->target();
    if ($attribute->parameter() != "") { echo "($", $attribute->parameter(), ")"; }
    echo " fields=", Core\Arr::count($attribute->fields()), "\n";
}

// `field` is the acting door, on `Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::constant`'s terms:
// naming a field reads the program's shape and always answers, reading one asks
// for the fold. Encoded rather than echoed so the value's *type* is asserted
// beside it — a fold that answered `"7"` for `7` passes neither half.
foreach ($roster as Core\Reflect\AttributeInfo $one) {
    foreach ($one->fields() as string $field) {
        echo "  ", $field, "=", Core\Json::encode($one->field($field)), "\n";
    }
}
--EXPECT--
5
Route on <class> fields=1
bare on <class> fields=1
bare on name fields=1
Route on show fields=1
bare on show($id) fields=1
  path="/users"
  audit=true
  column="name"
  path="/show"
  inject="repo"
